  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "When does a hybrid use its petrol engine?",
      "a": "The engine runs when more power is needed, at higher speeds, or when the battery is low. The computer starts and stops it automatically and keeps it working near its most efficient speed.",
      "asks": [
        "Does a hybrid engine run all the time?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What are power-split gears?",
      "a": "In many hybrids a planetary gear set links the engine, a generator and the drive motor. It can split the engine's power between driving the wheels and making electricity, in any mix.",
      "asks": [
        "How does a hybrid gearbox work?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Do you plug in a hybrid car?",
      "a": "A normal full hybrid is never plugged in: its small battery is charged by the engine and by braking. A plug-in hybrid has a bigger battery you can charge from the mains for longer electric-only driving.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a plug-in hybrid?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does a hybrid start off on electric power?",
      "a": "An electric motor gives strong turning force from standstill and burns no fuel, while a petrol engine is least efficient at low speed. So the motor handles pulling away.",
      "asks": [
        "Why is a hybrid silent when it pulls away?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does a hybrid recharge when braking?",
      "a": "When you lift off or brake, the electric motor is switched to work as a generator. Turning it takes energy from the car's movement, which slows the car and charges the battery.",
      "asks": [
        "What is regenerative braking in a hybrid?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "The Toyota Prius, launched in Japan in 1997, was the first mass-produced hybrid car.",
    "Full hybrids save the most fuel in stop-start town driving, where braking energy can be recovered often.",
    "A planetary gear set lets many hybrids mix engine and motor power without a normal gearbox."
  ],
